+# Wire UI Builder × Intention Service meeting point
+
+This recovery increment adds a design-time seam for the newer ooRexx Intention Service without making the Builder depend on an LLM, provider, database, or a specific application domain.
+
+## Authority split
+
+The Builder authors a `WireUIIntentionRequirement`. It may state:
+
+- `bindingId` — stable design identity;
+- `purposeRef` — provider-neutral semantic purpose;
+- `sphereRef` — optional intention/domain sphere;
+- `action` — an action already declared by the semantic element;
+- `discoveryPolicy=DYNAMIC`;
+- `readinessPolicy=READY_REQUIRED`;
+- `refreshPoint` — `BEFORE_OFFER`, `BEFORE_DISPATCH`, or `BEFORE_OFFER_AND_DISPATCH`.
+
+The Builder does **not** seal discovered registrations, provider identities, model confidence, resolved slots, evidence facts, feasibility results, or READY decisions.
+
+At runtime, Wire UI Server/application integration may use the requirement as an input to Intention Service discovery. Discovery must be refreshed at the declared point. An intention result can change while the compiled Wire UI release remains unchanged.
+
+`READY_REQUIRED` is deliberately stronger than recognition. A proposal or understood meaning must not directly produce the bound Wire UI action. The runtime authority path remains responsible for Intention Service policy/feasibility and ordinary Wire UI action availability/authorization.
+
+## Renderer agreement
+
+Web, Swing, GTK, Android and direct Linux renderers receive the same intention requirement as semantic definition metadata. They may present affordance state differently, but none may:
+
+- perform intention discovery independently as application authority;
+- cache a READY result as permanent truth;
+- infer permission from confidence;
+- replace `purposeRef` with renderer-specific commands;
+- dispatch the bound action until runtime authority reports it READY and the Wire UI action itself is available.
+
+A renderer may show unavailable, discovering, clarification-needed, or ready presentation if the authoritative runtime projects such state.
+
+## Backward compatibility
+
+A semantic element with zero intention requirements preserves the historical v0.6 canonical identity text. This prevents the addition of the optional intentions seam from changing all pre-existing element content addresses.
