+
+## Files
+
+- `src/WireUIWebControlCatalogue.cls` — reusable generic Builder component catalogue.
+- `examples/notnotes/NotNotesWebWireFixture.cls` — consumer-specific semantic fixture.
+- `web/notnotes/preview.html` — modern static projection checkpoint.
+- `web/notnotes/notnotes.css` — responsive presentation only.
+- `web/notnotes/notnotes-wire-preview.js` — preview action adapter; no application authority or persistence.
+- `tests/test_web_control_catalogue.rex` — generic catalogue contract.
+- `tests/test_notnotes_web_wire_fixture.rex` — downstream reference-fixture contract; intentionally not part of the mandatory consumer-neutral suite.
+
+
+## NotNotes v0.4 / dev16 compatibility increment (2026-10-15)
+
+The canonical NotNotes v0.4 graph now identifies Mini Application dev16, Windows Client dev2 and Intention Service dev11. The exact dev16/dev2 source archives were not materialised in the Library at this qualification point, so this package does not claim byte-level requalification against them.
+
+The Web semantic contract is nevertheless advanced at the documented boundaries:
+
+- `DATETIME_FIELD` carries value, timezone, resolution and detached temporal evidence.
+- `TEMPORAL_CONTEXT` is read-only presentation of authoritative civil/instant/timezone resolution. Unknown/ambiguous time is not guessed by the renderer.
+- `OBSERVATION_STATUS` exposes optional freshness/status evidence only; instrumentation cannot mutate or satisfy NotNotes actions.
+- `NotNotesWebProjectionPort` accepts optional `semanticProvider` and `observationProvider` adapters. They remain host-side authorities; the browser receives detached semantic projection only.
+- dynamic intention requirements remain `DYNAMIC` + `READY_REQUIRED` and refresh before offer and dispatch, compatible with the current Intention Service dev11 doctrine.
+
+The canonical architecture states that NotNotes dev16 normalizes DATE/DATETIME through Single Time Service and emits application/persistence/batch lifecycle observations through Instrumentation Mesh. Missing instrumentation leaves application semantics unchanged; ambiguous temporal resolution fails explicitly rather than guessing an offset.
