+# Resource executable objects -> Wire UI authoring bridge
+
+This increment consumes the current Windows-first RC executable-tree contract without making ooDialog, Win32 IDs, HWNDs or Windows messages part of the Wire application model.
+
+## Boundary
+
+```text
+Windows RC / resource source
+        |
+        v
+RC parser / executable resource tree
+  id + occurrence uid + properties + bound events
+        |
+        v
+WireUIResourceBridge
+        |
+        +--> Wire semantic element identity
+        +--> resource identity retained as provenance
+        +--> bound resource event -> Wire semantic action
+        +--> trusted handler name retained as provenance
+        |
+        v
+Wire Builder / renderer-neutral release
+```
+
+The source resource object owns resource facts. After import, Wire owns the authored Wire element identity. Native handles are never imported.
+
+## Identity
+
+Three identities deliberately remain distinct:
+
+1. **Wire element ID** — generated, unique Builder/application identity.
+2. **Resource ID** — e.g. `IDC_SAVE`; preserved as `metadata.resourceId`.
+3. **Occurrence UID** — e.g. `root/r1/e4`; preserved as `metadata.resourceUid`.
+
+The occurrence UID is essential because Windows resource IDs can legally repeat (`IDC_STATIC` is the obvious case). The current RC executable-tree line documents that occurrence UIDs are deterministic for an unchanged ordered tree but are not stable across arbitrary insertion/reordering. Consequently the first import is not a magical long-term identity synchronizer. A future resource reconciliation service must use provenance/evidence rather than pretending an occurrence path is permanently stable.
+
+## Trigger bindings
+
+Only trigger bindings actually published by the resource executable tree are imported. Each becomes:
+
+```text
+resource event: click
+Wire action:    RESOURCE.EVENT.CLICK
+handler:        saveCustomer   (provenance, not browser executable code)
+```
+
+The bridge does **not** invent unimplemented Win32 notification equivalence. The resource project currently lists callback signatures, notifications, command routing, focus and modality equivalence as open Windows coverage gates. As that authority publishes richer event slots, the bridge can consume them without changing the authority split.
+
+No RC/JSON text is evaluated as ooRexx source by this bridge.
